WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.350
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:06.590 --> 00:00:08.330
CRAIG GANSSLE:
Today, we're going

00:00:08.330 --> 00:00:12.470
to talk about how artificial
intelligence and how

00:00:12.470 --> 00:00:15.050
my company is working
with Google to help

00:00:15.050 --> 00:00:17.819
power global food production.

00:00:17.819 --> 00:00:18.860
My name is Craig Ganssle.

00:00:18.860 --> 00:00:21.320
And I'm the founder and
CEO of a company called

00:00:21.320 --> 00:00:23.460
CAMP3 out of Atlanta, Georgia.

00:00:23.460 --> 00:00:25.000
We have a product
called FARMWAVE.

00:00:28.860 --> 00:00:32.549
Artificial intelligence
can play a huge role

00:00:32.549 --> 00:00:37.240
in important industries, such
as global food production,

00:00:37.240 --> 00:00:39.900
just to name a few.

00:00:39.900 --> 00:00:43.370
If I were to ask
you, "What is this?"

00:00:43.370 --> 00:00:45.370
You would tell me it's an apple.

00:00:45.370 --> 00:00:49.730
But what if I said,
now prove it to me?

00:00:49.730 --> 00:00:52.070
Prove to me that
this is an apple.

00:00:52.070 --> 00:00:54.140
How do you know that?

00:00:54.140 --> 00:00:57.920
Because when we were kids,
preschool or kindergarten,

00:00:57.920 --> 00:00:59.510
we all saw the same
thing, the little

00:00:59.510 --> 00:01:01.490
flashcard with the picture.

00:01:01.490 --> 00:01:02.500
And it said apple.

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:08.460
What about that?

00:01:08.460 --> 00:01:11.020
That's an apple too.

00:01:11.020 --> 00:01:13.750
But it doesn't look like that.

00:01:13.750 --> 00:01:17.090
So what do you
mean it's an apple?

00:01:17.090 --> 00:01:18.980
What about that one?

00:01:18.980 --> 00:01:21.320
That looks different too.

00:01:21.320 --> 00:01:22.970
Or that?

00:01:22.970 --> 00:01:25.870
These are all apples.

00:01:25.870 --> 00:01:29.560
But in global food production,
using artificial intelligence,

00:01:29.560 --> 00:01:31.120
we have to prove it.

00:01:31.120 --> 00:01:33.260
And not only would
we have to prove it,

00:01:33.260 --> 00:01:36.460
that it's an apple, but
all the pathogen, pest,

00:01:36.460 --> 00:01:42.260
and weed infestations that
could infect any crop out there.

00:01:42.260 --> 00:01:44.650
Welcome to the challenges
of AI in plant pathology

00:01:44.650 --> 00:01:46.730
in agriculture.

00:01:46.730 --> 00:01:51.580
It's a daunting task to use
things like image recognition

00:01:51.580 --> 00:01:56.680
to identify pathogen and
pest infestation in crops

00:01:56.680 --> 00:01:58.480
or plants--

00:01:58.480 --> 00:02:03.280
tomatoes, citrus, row
crops, anything-- early on.

00:02:03.280 --> 00:02:05.470
But this is one of the
most critical stages

00:02:05.470 --> 00:02:07.060
in growing food.

00:02:07.060 --> 00:02:11.080
If we can identify pathogen and
pest infestation early enough,

00:02:11.080 --> 00:02:13.030
that means we could
spray less chemicals.

00:02:13.030 --> 00:02:15.910
That means we have less crop
destruction, bigger crop

00:02:15.910 --> 00:02:17.260
yields.

00:02:17.260 --> 00:02:19.210
That could mean more food.

00:02:19.210 --> 00:02:22.510
This is an industry that is
really important globally

00:02:22.510 --> 00:02:26.280
because we all need to eat.

00:02:26.280 --> 00:02:29.340
Dr. Fei-Fei Li has said it best.

00:02:29.340 --> 00:02:31.120
Build a new dataset.

00:02:31.120 --> 00:02:34.630
And so at FARMWAVE, that's
what we set out to do.

00:02:34.630 --> 00:02:36.510
We have searched all
around the world--

00:02:36.510 --> 00:02:41.530
universities, large stakeholder
corporations, farmers,

00:02:41.530 --> 00:02:44.280
smallholders-- to
capture imagery and build

00:02:44.280 --> 00:02:47.100
a dataset that is
curated and cataloged

00:02:47.100 --> 00:02:50.120
for image recognition
for plant pathology

00:02:50.120 --> 00:02:53.830
and reducing crop loss.

00:02:53.830 --> 00:02:56.590
FARMWAVE is an app that
connects people in agriculture

00:02:56.590 --> 00:02:59.750
with their farm and
technology and each other.

00:02:59.750 --> 00:03:01.960
It's more than just the
artificial intelligence,

00:03:01.960 --> 00:03:03.670
but that's the real
powerhouse behind it.

00:03:06.930 --> 00:03:09.540
In the past couple years that
we've been developing FARMWAVE,

00:03:09.540 --> 00:03:12.210
we've done pilots and proof
of concepts around the world--

00:03:12.210 --> 00:03:16.770
with large corporations, such
as Monsanto, Syngenta, Bayer,

00:03:16.770 --> 00:03:20.550
small farmers from India,
France, the United States,

00:03:20.550 --> 00:03:21.720
all around the world--

00:03:21.720 --> 00:03:24.240
and found out that if
we could get FARMWAVE

00:03:24.240 --> 00:03:27.997
into the hands of farmers
all around the world,

00:03:27.997 --> 00:03:29.580
we have a really
good shot at reducing

00:03:29.580 --> 00:03:31.800
crop destruction by 20% to 30%.

00:03:35.430 --> 00:03:39.750
One of the components is
communities, enabling farmers

00:03:39.750 --> 00:03:41.700
to talk to each other.

00:03:41.700 --> 00:03:44.650
Today, they use things
like Twitter and Instagram.

00:03:44.650 --> 00:03:47.070
But in their day-to-day
job, and how fast they move,

00:03:47.070 --> 00:03:50.730
and all the tasks that
have to happen on the farm,

00:03:50.730 --> 00:03:52.600
they're using Twitter
and Instagram.

00:03:52.600 --> 00:03:55.410
But all the noise of the news
and everything else they follow

00:03:55.410 --> 00:03:57.810
is kind of getting
stuck in the middle--

00:03:57.810 --> 00:03:59.490
not to mention you
have some growers who

00:03:59.490 --> 00:04:01.650
grow for large
stakeholder companies

00:04:01.650 --> 00:04:03.180
where they're
doing plant trials,

00:04:03.180 --> 00:04:05.221
and that information is
proprietary and shouldn't

00:04:05.221 --> 00:04:06.450
be out on the public web.

00:04:06.450 --> 00:04:08.790
FARMWAVE gives them a
closed-loop community

00:04:08.790 --> 00:04:11.640
where researchers,
agronomists, and farmers alike

00:04:11.640 --> 00:04:13.140
can collaborate
by building field

00:04:13.140 --> 00:04:16.579
reports in near real-time.

00:04:16.579 --> 00:04:18.399
This allows us to
have the data right

00:04:18.399 --> 00:04:20.149
in the palm of their hands.

00:04:20.149 --> 00:04:23.860
There's a lot happening
in agriculture today.

00:04:23.860 --> 00:04:26.140
All around the world,
there's different companies

00:04:26.140 --> 00:04:29.650
that are making attempts
at autonomous vehicles.

00:04:29.650 --> 00:04:32.300
John Deere's been doing
it for a very long time.

00:04:32.300 --> 00:04:35.110
We've had autonomous
tractors for decades.

00:04:35.110 --> 00:04:37.840
They move a lot slower, and
there's nobody in their way.

00:04:37.840 --> 00:04:41.230
But they've been
doing it for a while.

00:04:41.230 --> 00:04:43.600
Unmanned aerial vehicles,
satellite imagery,

00:04:43.600 --> 00:04:46.900
hyperspectral color
imagery, all of this data

00:04:46.900 --> 00:04:49.060
is very important
to agriculture.

00:04:49.060 --> 00:04:50.650
And there are many,
many companies

00:04:50.650 --> 00:04:53.830
around the world trying to make
an impact into how we could

00:04:53.830 --> 00:04:55.900
better make use of
all of this data

00:04:55.900 --> 00:04:59.380
to make better decisions
in our farming.

00:04:59.380 --> 00:05:03.010
Howard Buffett wrote
the book "40 Chances."

00:05:03.010 --> 00:05:05.650
If you think about it, a
farmer has in their lifetime

00:05:05.650 --> 00:05:09.070
an average of 40
chances to get it right.

00:05:09.070 --> 00:05:10.860
And that's it.

00:05:10.860 --> 00:05:13.200
Because everything that
they do, their day-to-day,

00:05:13.200 --> 00:05:14.700
is very seasonal.

00:05:14.700 --> 00:05:17.070
Everything plants in the
spring, they harvest and get

00:05:17.070 --> 00:05:18.630
the results in the fall.

00:05:18.630 --> 00:05:20.880
Whatever didn't work, they
can try it again next year.

00:05:24.120 --> 00:05:27.240
A farmer in Indiana,
Chris Tom of Tom Farms,

00:05:27.240 --> 00:05:29.730
they farm about 45,000 acres.

00:05:29.730 --> 00:05:32.880
They're one of the largest
growers in the United States.

00:05:32.880 --> 00:05:34.680
He said something to me once.

00:05:34.680 --> 00:05:37.230
Don't add more technology on
top of existing technology

00:05:37.230 --> 00:05:40.990
for the sake of
adding new technology.

00:05:40.990 --> 00:05:43.260
So we're trying to train
things from the ground

00:05:43.260 --> 00:05:45.399
up with FARMWAVE and our
artificial intelligence

00:05:45.399 --> 00:05:47.940
to work with the devices that
they're already carrying around

00:05:47.940 --> 00:05:49.890
with them or that they're
already using today,

00:05:49.890 --> 00:05:53.336
such as unmanned aerial vehicles
and even some of the machinery.

00:05:56.670 --> 00:05:59.100
We leverage a lot
of technologies

00:05:59.100 --> 00:06:02.580
within Google Cloud Platform.

00:06:02.580 --> 00:06:05.970
Recently, we've been one of
the alpha companies working

00:06:05.970 --> 00:06:07.830
with AutoML.

00:06:07.830 --> 00:06:10.950
And we have a partnership
with the University of Georgia

00:06:10.950 --> 00:06:14.770
agriculture extension-- which
is where we are from, Atlanta--

00:06:14.770 --> 00:06:17.640
where we had what is called the
Consortium for Internet Imagery

00:06:17.640 --> 00:06:19.760
Database System.

00:06:19.760 --> 00:06:22.980
Owned by University of Georgia,
and about 19 other universities

00:06:22.980 --> 00:06:25.320
around the world
have participated in,

00:06:25.320 --> 00:06:30.960
it is 20 years of imagery data
on over 35,000 plant species.

00:06:30.960 --> 00:06:33.380
So we've worked closely with
the University of Georgia.

00:06:33.380 --> 00:06:34.838
And we got a copy
of this database.

00:06:34.838 --> 00:06:37.350
And we started
classifying, and labeling,

00:06:37.350 --> 00:06:41.000
and tagging, and running
imagery through FARMWAVE.

00:06:41.000 --> 00:06:42.990
It's been very,
very challenging.

00:06:42.990 --> 00:06:46.639
20, 19, 18 years ago, we didn't
have the kind of mobile phones

00:06:46.639 --> 00:06:47.430
that we have today.

00:06:47.430 --> 00:06:48.971
So the pictures
really aren't usable.

00:06:48.971 --> 00:06:51.490
They're not a very good quality.

00:06:51.490 --> 00:06:54.550
But as we started to
see more recent imagery,

00:06:54.550 --> 00:06:55.780
we have better quality.

00:06:55.780 --> 00:06:56.950
Some are good pictures.

00:06:56.950 --> 00:06:58.132
Some are not.

00:06:58.132 --> 00:07:00.340
In plant pathology, you have
to get up really, really

00:07:00.340 --> 00:07:02.590
close and get a really
good picture of a leaf

00:07:02.590 --> 00:07:04.540
to get really early
detection where you'll

00:07:04.540 --> 00:07:07.660
have an impact that you can
identify a pathogen or a pest,

00:07:07.660 --> 00:07:09.619
make a recommendation
and a treatment,

00:07:09.619 --> 00:07:10.910
and put the input in the field.

00:07:14.180 --> 00:07:19.710
We also got early on
into counting sequences.

00:07:19.710 --> 00:07:22.410
Today, farmers will
help predict yield

00:07:22.410 --> 00:07:25.640
by counting the kernels
on an ear of corn.

00:07:25.640 --> 00:07:28.020
They do that with a Sharpie.

00:07:28.020 --> 00:07:30.530
Now, I don't know how many of
you have spent time farming.

00:07:30.530 --> 00:07:33.410
But if you are in the
south Georgia heat in July

00:07:33.410 --> 00:07:36.020
with a Sharpie, and you're
trying to count kernels

00:07:36.020 --> 00:07:39.002
on an ear of corn, it's awful.

00:07:39.002 --> 00:07:40.460
And by the time
you roll it around,

00:07:40.460 --> 00:07:43.460
some of the sweat in your
hands washed off the marker.

00:07:43.460 --> 00:07:47.050
And you don't really have
a good, accurate count.

00:07:47.050 --> 00:07:50.890
So using some of the
Vision API technology,

00:07:50.890 --> 00:07:52.810
we've developed a
system that allows

00:07:52.810 --> 00:07:56.830
you to take a picture of the ear
of corn, rotate it 180 degrees,

00:07:56.830 --> 00:07:57.920
take another picture.

00:07:57.920 --> 00:07:59.920
And it counts it in
about two seconds.

00:07:59.920 --> 00:08:02.770
We've been 95-plus percent
accurate on these counts

00:08:02.770 --> 00:08:05.980
consecutively-- indoors,
outdoors, regardless

00:08:05.980 --> 00:08:07.700
of different light levels.

00:08:07.700 --> 00:08:10.900
This is an increase
by 17% to 20%

00:08:10.900 --> 00:08:13.660
of what was acceptable,
according to the Iowa Corn

00:08:13.660 --> 00:08:15.204
Board.

00:08:15.204 --> 00:08:16.620
Later this year,
we're going to be

00:08:16.620 --> 00:08:18.411
launching what's in
the middle image, which

00:08:18.411 --> 00:08:20.770
is called Stand Counts.

00:08:20.770 --> 00:08:24.220
Stand Counts allow farmers
to know how many they

00:08:24.220 --> 00:08:26.560
have in a given row.

00:08:26.560 --> 00:08:28.900
Sometimes, this imagery
can be taken by UAVs.

00:08:28.900 --> 00:08:31.330
Sometimes it's
taken on the ground.

00:08:31.330 --> 00:08:34.240
Cluster counts in
tomatoes, wine grapes

00:08:34.240 --> 00:08:36.690
helping to predict yield.

00:08:36.690 --> 00:08:39.539
The more decisions that
farmers can make earlier on,

00:08:39.539 --> 00:08:43.929
the better it is for overall
global food production.

00:08:43.929 --> 00:08:47.710
The image recognition
for us was about time.

00:08:47.710 --> 00:08:50.140
Now, let me give you an idea
of what it would start like.

00:08:50.140 --> 00:08:52.950
Here's how it's
typically done today.

00:08:52.950 --> 00:08:54.880
A farmer goes out
into the field.

00:08:54.880 --> 00:08:56.834
Or, satellite takes a picture.

00:08:56.834 --> 00:08:58.000
Or, a drone takes a picture.

00:08:58.000 --> 00:09:00.070
And they see that
there's a distress

00:09:00.070 --> 00:09:02.471
in an area of the field.

00:09:02.471 --> 00:09:04.970
Then, they'll send somebody
out, and they'll take a picture.

00:09:04.970 --> 00:09:06.386
Maybe the farmer
knows what it is.

00:09:06.386 --> 00:09:07.790
Maybe they don't.

00:09:07.790 --> 00:09:11.210
Because even today, we still
see new diseases that pop up.

00:09:11.210 --> 00:09:13.310
So they say, OK, I don't
really know what this is.

00:09:13.310 --> 00:09:15.650
I've got to get this
to an agronomist.

00:09:15.650 --> 00:09:18.440
But I live out in
the middle of nowhere

00:09:18.440 --> 00:09:21.850
in Iowa in a sea of corn.

00:09:21.850 --> 00:09:25.130
The nearest university
is four hours away.

00:09:25.130 --> 00:09:27.400
So I make a phone call
to my local agronomist.

00:09:27.400 --> 00:09:29.050
He's two states away.

00:09:29.050 --> 00:09:32.371
He'll get to me maybe next week.

00:09:32.371 --> 00:09:33.870
So he reaches out
to the university.

00:09:33.870 --> 00:09:36.180
They say, we can get
somebody to come out and take

00:09:36.180 --> 00:09:38.660
a look in a couple of days.

00:09:38.660 --> 00:09:39.460
So they come out.

00:09:39.460 --> 00:09:40.364
They take a look.

00:09:40.364 --> 00:09:42.530
If they know what it is,
they make a recommendation.

00:09:42.530 --> 00:09:43.780
They write a prescription.

00:09:43.780 --> 00:09:45.430
And the farmer
calls their dealer.

00:09:45.430 --> 00:09:48.310
And they come out and put down
either an herbicide, fungicide,

00:09:48.310 --> 00:09:49.912
pesticide, whatever it might be.

00:09:49.912 --> 00:09:51.370
If they don't know
what it is, they

00:09:51.370 --> 00:09:53.330
have to do some
further research.

00:09:53.330 --> 00:09:56.690
Maybe that takes a couple
more days, maybe another week.

00:09:56.690 --> 00:10:00.940
Depending on climate, weather,
traits, whatever they have,

00:10:00.940 --> 00:10:02.200
this could spread.

00:10:02.200 --> 00:10:04.720
This could spread from
5% of their crop to 20%

00:10:04.720 --> 00:10:10.740
to 30% to 40%, decimating
their yield, losing food.

00:10:10.740 --> 00:10:12.930
For us, it's about time.

00:10:12.930 --> 00:10:15.300
FARMWAVE and the image
recognition capability

00:10:15.300 --> 00:10:18.420
makes this happen in
a matter of seconds.

00:10:18.420 --> 00:10:21.420
Now, 35,000 species
from the University

00:10:21.420 --> 00:10:25.650
of Georgia, 20 years of crop
imagery, we are just getting

00:10:25.650 --> 00:10:26.850
started.

00:10:26.850 --> 00:10:28.950
This is a huge database.

00:10:28.950 --> 00:10:31.680
Corn alone and all
the diseases in corn

00:10:31.680 --> 00:10:36.510
has taken us several years just
to kind of get under our belt.

00:10:36.510 --> 00:10:39.040
Because if you remember the
pictures from the apples,

00:10:39.040 --> 00:10:42.870
we have to look at a crop in
all of its different stages

00:10:42.870 --> 00:10:46.350
and every disease for that crop
in all of its different stages

00:10:46.350 --> 00:10:51.660
from early on to 20% severe,
60% severe, fully decimated

00:10:51.660 --> 00:10:53.730
to get accurate results.

00:10:53.730 --> 00:10:56.184
Our library of imagery
is one of the only one

00:10:56.184 --> 00:10:57.600
we know of today
that we have been

00:10:57.600 --> 00:10:59.220
told that has been
properly validated

00:10:59.220 --> 00:11:01.440
by PhD level pathologists
and entomologists

00:11:01.440 --> 00:11:03.700
from around the world.

00:11:03.700 --> 00:11:06.401
But we have to
have high accuracy.

00:11:06.401 --> 00:11:07.400
So we have to have that.

00:11:07.400 --> 00:11:08.358
But it takes more time.

00:11:11.190 --> 00:11:13.860
When farmers go out to a field--

00:11:13.860 --> 00:11:17.580
if you've been through farmland,
you'll see these placards.

00:11:17.580 --> 00:11:22.020
It tells us what kind of crop
is being planted right there.

00:11:22.020 --> 00:11:24.720
In this case, it's
a Pioneer version.

00:11:24.720 --> 00:11:27.330
Using the Google
Vision API, farmers

00:11:27.330 --> 00:11:29.640
are able to very quickly
fill out their field report

00:11:29.640 --> 00:11:31.860
by simply taking a
picture, extracting

00:11:31.860 --> 00:11:33.390
the information
from the placard,

00:11:33.390 --> 00:11:35.011
and putting it into
the field report.

00:11:35.011 --> 00:11:37.260
Then they continue that field
report when they get out

00:11:37.260 --> 00:11:39.930
of their vehicle, and they go
into the field to actually look

00:11:39.930 --> 00:11:42.330
at a pathogen or pest problem.

00:11:42.330 --> 00:11:44.370
This saves a lot of time.

00:11:44.370 --> 00:11:47.010
Maybe it's just a few
minutes a day for a farmer.

00:11:47.010 --> 00:11:51.630
But that compounds for farmers
around the world every year.

00:11:51.630 --> 00:11:53.650
This sort of
information is critical.

00:11:53.650 --> 00:11:57.690
It has to be filed with state
regulatory and local county

00:11:57.690 --> 00:11:59.464
reports.

00:11:59.464 --> 00:12:01.380
So this information is
important to get right.

00:12:01.380 --> 00:12:04.200
The Google Vision API has
helped us tremendously

00:12:04.200 --> 00:12:07.120
in extracting this
information very quickly when

00:12:07.120 --> 00:12:09.182
going to the field.

00:12:09.182 --> 00:12:10.890
This is a little bit
of a look at a field

00:12:10.890 --> 00:12:13.980
report inside FARMWAVE.

00:12:13.980 --> 00:12:16.650
We help them by recognizing
the pathogens and pests.

00:12:16.650 --> 00:12:18.570
But we leverage everything
in Google Cloud,

00:12:18.570 --> 00:12:23.230
where FARMWAVE lives, to get
information in near real-time.

00:12:23.230 --> 00:12:25.290
We also geotag where they are.

00:12:25.290 --> 00:12:26.550
It's very important.

00:12:26.550 --> 00:12:28.170
When they go out
to a field, what

00:12:28.170 --> 00:12:29.430
has happened in this field?

00:12:29.430 --> 00:12:32.400
We tag weather, what the
weather was, where they are,

00:12:32.400 --> 00:12:34.530
time, and date.

00:12:34.530 --> 00:12:36.380
The future of
FARMWAVE next year is

00:12:36.380 --> 00:12:40.160
to launch an app store in 2019
that allows other machinery

00:12:40.160 --> 00:12:43.040
and data points from
all within agriculture

00:12:43.040 --> 00:12:45.920
to connect to FARMWAVE so
we can correlate that data

00:12:45.920 --> 00:12:48.300
and collaborate better.

00:12:48.300 --> 00:12:50.660
That means if a farmer
goes back out to a field

00:12:50.660 --> 00:12:53.400
and opens up FARMWAVE
to take a field report,

00:12:53.400 --> 00:12:54.780
it sees where they are.

00:12:54.780 --> 00:12:58.220
And it says, let me pull
your John Deere planter

00:12:58.220 --> 00:12:59.810
because I know where you are.

00:12:59.810 --> 00:13:01.910
And I will tell you
in a few seconds what

00:13:01.910 --> 00:13:05.450
was planted in this field,
on what date, what seed, what

00:13:05.450 --> 00:13:08.720
variety, at what spacing,
at what compaction.

00:13:08.720 --> 00:13:10.937
And then let me pull your
irrigation sensor data.

00:13:10.937 --> 00:13:12.770
And I will tell you how
much water this crop

00:13:12.770 --> 00:13:14.840
has had from the day
it's gone into the ground

00:13:14.840 --> 00:13:17.900
until this very moment based off
exactly where you're standing.

00:13:17.900 --> 00:13:19.040
It pushes those results.

00:13:19.040 --> 00:13:20.750
It puts them in
the field report.

00:13:20.750 --> 00:13:23.900
Then you can take pictures,
notes, video, dictation

00:13:23.900 --> 00:13:24.710
and save it.

00:13:24.710 --> 00:13:26.760
And when you save it,
in those communities.

00:13:26.760 --> 00:13:29.810
I mentioned earlier, it
gets shared right away

00:13:29.810 --> 00:13:33.260
with your agronomists,
researchers, growers, dealers

00:13:33.260 --> 00:13:35.575
anywhere in the world
in near real-time.

00:13:38.202 --> 00:13:39.660
A few years ago,
I had the pleasure

00:13:39.660 --> 00:13:45.100
of visiting with President
Emmanuel Macron of France.

00:13:45.100 --> 00:13:50.370
France is the capital of the
world in wine and cheese.

00:13:50.370 --> 00:13:54.360
And it was fantastic to see
how they grow differently

00:13:54.360 --> 00:13:57.430
in France than they do
here in the United States.

00:13:57.430 --> 00:14:00.164
And I've been to Amman,
Jordan, and Beirut, Lebanon,

00:14:00.164 --> 00:14:02.580
and how they grow differently
there than they do in France

00:14:02.580 --> 00:14:05.720
than they do in
the United States.

00:14:05.720 --> 00:14:09.060
France is one of those countries
that GMOs are prohibited.

00:14:09.060 --> 00:14:10.520
So they have different styles.

00:14:10.520 --> 00:14:13.010
But data is still as important.

00:14:13.010 --> 00:14:17.060
And farmers all around
France work together

00:14:17.060 --> 00:14:19.940
through subsidies
from the government

00:14:19.940 --> 00:14:22.520
for new technology every year.

00:14:22.520 --> 00:14:24.920
We've been visiting with
France the past couple years

00:14:24.920 --> 00:14:28.670
several times, working with
different co-ops, growers,

00:14:28.670 --> 00:14:32.090
and even with the government on
how FARMWAVE could potentially

00:14:32.090 --> 00:14:38.420
be the single platform that the
country uses to aggregate data

00:14:38.420 --> 00:14:40.790
and do predictive
modeling in the future.

00:14:40.790 --> 00:14:42.740
Because as we look
at AI, and as we

00:14:42.740 --> 00:14:45.080
look at machine learning
and deep learning

00:14:45.080 --> 00:14:48.850
and what we can leverage
Google Cloud Platform to do,

00:14:48.850 --> 00:14:52.520
it's about those 40 chances that
Howard Buffett talked about.

00:14:52.520 --> 00:14:56.650
It's about, what
can we do and learn

00:14:56.650 --> 00:15:00.232
for the future based off the
knowledge that we get today?

00:15:00.232 --> 00:15:02.190
I just had a conversation
recently with a lady,

00:15:02.190 --> 00:15:04.020
Dr. Suzanne Wainwright-Evans.

00:15:04.020 --> 00:15:08.900
She's a world-renowned
entomologist, Buglady.

00:15:08.900 --> 00:15:15.170
She said, I'm pretty skeptical
about FARMWAVE, Craig.

00:15:15.170 --> 00:15:17.850
I come from decades
of experience.

00:15:17.850 --> 00:15:20.690
And that experience and
what I do in the field,

00:15:20.690 --> 00:15:23.180
we call biases in AI.

00:15:23.180 --> 00:15:25.070
And she's absolutely right.

00:15:25.070 --> 00:15:26.360
There's a lot of biases.

00:15:26.360 --> 00:15:28.880
There's a lot of things that
entomologists will just see

00:15:28.880 --> 00:15:33.260
and do just as their nature from
being an entomologist for years

00:15:33.260 --> 00:15:35.990
that are going to be really hard
for us to train in the system.

00:15:35.990 --> 00:15:38.720
And we're working
very hard on that.

00:15:38.720 --> 00:15:41.900
But the fact is Dr.
Suzanne Wainwright-Evans

00:15:41.900 --> 00:15:44.030
will not be here forever.

00:15:44.030 --> 00:15:47.480
And today, it's not very
popular to be the next Buglady.

00:15:47.480 --> 00:15:51.000
So we don't have as
many entomologists.

00:15:51.000 --> 00:15:55.740
In 2017 alone, Dr. Evans
spent three weeks at home.

00:15:55.740 --> 00:15:57.500
The rest was on the road.

00:15:57.500 --> 00:16:00.052
She is extremely sought after
in other countries, where

00:16:00.052 --> 00:16:02.010
they see pest infestations
that they don't even

00:16:02.010 --> 00:16:02.920
know what it is.

00:16:02.920 --> 00:16:06.480
And they need her
help to save crops.

00:16:06.480 --> 00:16:09.000
So working with
somebody like Dr. Evans

00:16:09.000 --> 00:16:12.420
to help train our machines,
to help train our AI

00:16:12.420 --> 00:16:15.960
to know what we're looking at
in pathogen or pest infestation,

00:16:15.960 --> 00:16:19.380
is critical to preserve her
knowledge of the past couple

00:16:19.380 --> 00:16:21.180
decades for the
future of agriculture

00:16:21.180 --> 00:16:23.506
tomorrow so we can
better make decisions.

00:16:26.719 --> 00:16:28.760
These are some of the
partners that we work with.

00:16:28.760 --> 00:16:31.220
Obviously, I talked about
the University of Georgia.

00:16:31.220 --> 00:16:35.690
We did some early work
with Google X. FARMWAVE

00:16:35.690 --> 00:16:39.450
actually began on Google Glass.

00:16:39.450 --> 00:16:41.874
I was an early adopter
of Google Glass.

00:16:41.874 --> 00:16:42.790
I went up to New York.

00:16:42.790 --> 00:16:46.270
I was asked to come up there
and get a pair of Google Glass.

00:16:46.270 --> 00:16:47.820
And a few months
after I got it, I

00:16:47.820 --> 00:16:51.560
went to a John Deere developer
conference in Des Moines, Iowa.

00:16:51.560 --> 00:16:53.620
And John Deere had
invited their developers

00:16:53.620 --> 00:16:56.080
from all around the world to
come in for this conference.

00:16:56.080 --> 00:17:00.130
And it was kind of a, what do
you have that's new for us?

00:17:00.130 --> 00:17:02.380
And the developers were sort
of looking at John Deere,

00:17:02.380 --> 00:17:04.490
saying, well, what do you
have that's new for us?

00:17:04.490 --> 00:17:05.589
This one gentleman
came up to me.

00:17:05.589 --> 00:17:07.480
And he said, Craig, would you
get up and talk about Google

00:17:07.480 --> 00:17:08.639
Glass and what it's doing?

00:17:08.639 --> 00:17:10.930
Because a lot of people have
asked us about it and what

00:17:10.930 --> 00:17:11.430
it is.

00:17:11.430 --> 00:17:12.970
I said, sure.

00:17:12.970 --> 00:17:15.760
At this point in time,
FARMWAVE wasn't even born yet.

00:17:15.760 --> 00:17:18.344
We had just gotten up and
talked about Glass and what

00:17:18.344 --> 00:17:19.510
it was doing out of the box.

00:17:19.510 --> 00:17:21.485
And this was with
the Explorer edition.

00:17:21.485 --> 00:17:23.109
And then as I learned
a little bit more

00:17:23.109 --> 00:17:25.540
about the critical problem
solving in agriculture,

00:17:25.540 --> 00:17:28.750
and we learned about the
operation of a crop scout

00:17:28.750 --> 00:17:31.060
to identify these
things, I knew that we

00:17:31.060 --> 00:17:33.700
could use image
recognition technology

00:17:33.700 --> 00:17:36.340
to make these problems
go away and make

00:17:36.340 --> 00:17:37.930
these decisions faster.

00:17:37.930 --> 00:17:40.210
So it actually began
on Google Glass.

00:17:40.210 --> 00:17:44.080
We did a pilot with
Monsanto in 2015

00:17:44.080 --> 00:17:48.270
on about 25 different pairs
of Google Glass in Illinois,

00:17:48.270 --> 00:17:50.500
where farmers were able
to go out to the field

00:17:50.500 --> 00:17:52.420
and simply use their
voice and take pictures.

00:17:52.420 --> 00:17:53.560
And it would identify.

00:17:53.560 --> 00:17:55.870
Or, they would have the
hands-free option because

00:17:55.870 --> 00:17:58.030
of Glass to hold it up
and count the kernels

00:17:58.030 --> 00:17:59.494
in a matter of seconds.

00:17:59.494 --> 00:18:00.910
So that's some of
the work that we

00:18:00.910 --> 00:18:06.820
did early on with Google X. It
still does work today on Glass.

00:18:06.820 --> 00:18:11.500
And actually, yesterday, we
launched FARMWAVE globally

00:18:11.500 --> 00:18:14.920
in the iOS and
Android app stores.

00:18:14.920 --> 00:18:17.390
I was at an ag tech conference
in Atlanta yesterday.

00:18:17.390 --> 00:18:19.090
And we did our global launch.

00:18:19.090 --> 00:18:22.120
And we've got 80
countries and about

00:18:22.120 --> 00:18:25.180
34,000 people in
the past 60 days

00:18:25.180 --> 00:18:28.660
who have signed up to
early adopt FARMWAVE.

00:18:28.660 --> 00:18:30.310
It's a critical problem.

00:18:30.310 --> 00:18:33.010
We are expected by 2050,
for the amount of people

00:18:33.010 --> 00:18:34.690
that will be on
the planet, we have

00:18:34.690 --> 00:18:37.120
to grow more food
in the next 30 years

00:18:37.120 --> 00:18:39.380
than we have in the last 8,000--

00:18:39.380 --> 00:18:45.070
with less resources, less
land, and more mouths to feed.

00:18:45.070 --> 00:18:47.370
Technology is going
to play a pivotal role

00:18:47.370 --> 00:18:49.160
in making this happen.

00:18:49.160 --> 00:18:51.660
And we're really glad
to be part of that.

00:18:51.660 --> 00:18:55.594
And we've not been able to do it
without the powerful resources

00:18:55.594 --> 00:18:56.260
of Google Cloud.

00:19:07.630 --> 00:19:12.550
Within the image recognition
that you see on this report 01,

00:19:12.550 --> 00:19:15.130
you'll see that there's
a map at the bottom.

00:19:15.130 --> 00:19:20.440
I also wanted to point out that
the geospatial capability, when

00:19:20.440 --> 00:19:23.960
you take a field report
to show where you are,

00:19:23.960 --> 00:19:26.270
that populates on a map.

00:19:26.270 --> 00:19:28.970
Now, if I'm in Iowa,
and I'm growing corn,

00:19:28.970 --> 00:19:30.680
and I see that I have
a problem, and I'm

00:19:30.680 --> 00:19:32.930
in one of these communities
with the Iowa Corn Growers

00:19:32.930 --> 00:19:35.270
Association, and
I take a picture,

00:19:35.270 --> 00:19:39.426
and I do a field report, and I
alert my neighboring farmers,

00:19:39.426 --> 00:19:41.050
they'll see where a
disease is actually

00:19:41.050 --> 00:19:43.420
spreading in near real-time.

00:19:43.420 --> 00:19:46.630
And through a
dashboard view, if you

00:19:46.630 --> 00:19:49.150
are an administrator of
your FARMWAVE community

00:19:49.150 --> 00:19:51.820
or your FARMWAVE
group, you can actually

00:19:51.820 --> 00:19:53.680
see all of the growers.

00:19:53.680 --> 00:19:59.200
And through different tags and
different tagging on the map,

00:19:59.200 --> 00:20:03.016
you can actually see it grow
and spread in real-time.

00:20:03.016 --> 00:20:04.390
This has an alert
effect that can

00:20:04.390 --> 00:20:06.070
alert other farmers
of what's happening

00:20:06.070 --> 00:20:08.140
and what's coming their way.

00:20:08.140 --> 00:20:11.140
And it allows, in that
community, for those farmers

00:20:11.140 --> 00:20:16.280
to communicate with others
around the world for advice.

00:20:16.280 --> 00:20:20.640
A dairy farmer in Wisconsin
who farms about 860 cows a day,

00:20:20.640 --> 00:20:25.640
three times a day, is pretty
advanced in what he's doing.

00:20:25.640 --> 00:20:30.080
But a dairy farmer in India
who only has eight cows

00:20:30.080 --> 00:20:32.660
doesn't have the knowledge or
the resources or the skillset

00:20:32.660 --> 00:20:35.090
that the gentleman
in Wisconsin does.

00:20:35.090 --> 00:20:39.440
Through FARMWAVE, he can
send pictures and ask advice.

00:20:39.440 --> 00:20:42.200
And because those pictures
are in a field report--

00:20:42.200 --> 00:20:45.065
that is, location- and
weather-tagged-- the farmer

00:20:45.065 --> 00:20:49.640
in Wisconsin can immediately
advise the farmer in India.

00:20:49.640 --> 00:20:52.220
I see that you have eight cows.

00:20:52.220 --> 00:20:54.252
You live in a very
humid climate.

00:20:54.252 --> 00:20:56.210
Here's the conditions
that you're dealing with.

00:20:56.210 --> 00:20:58.310
That's very likely mastitis.

00:20:58.310 --> 00:21:00.636
And here's what you
need to do about it.

00:21:00.636 --> 00:21:02.510
And that's critical to
farmers in other parts

00:21:02.510 --> 00:21:05.450
of the world in
growing countries

00:21:05.450 --> 00:21:09.950
where those eight cows is the
sustainability of their family.

00:21:09.950 --> 00:21:12.700
[MUSIC PLAYING]

